From dd800020d35c6dff3b0a50d6f1848f3371b94071 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:38:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] settings: videoscreen.hades3doutput, whether a 3D display is
 told of the layout

A setting for what the commits after this one do: while Kodi draws the
two eyes of a stereoscopic picture one above the other or side by side,
the display is given the mode that says so, or is told by the HDMI
vendor InfoFrame, and goes to 3D by itself. In System > Display >
Stereoscopic 3D, "Switch the display to 3D (HDMI)": off or automatic,
automatic by default. Nothing reads it yet.

Off: the two halves go out in an ordinary 2D mode and the display is
switched to 3D by hand, which is what Kodi did until now with every
driver that lists no 3D modes. (Where a driver lists them, as i915
does, they could be picked as resolutions; they are no longer among the
resolutions, whatever this setting says.)

As the other settings of Hades: on GBM with OpenGL ES, with English core
strings (#39978 the help and #39979 the label, the next free ones below
those in use) that are translated in Estuary (#31712 and #31711; Spanish
in es_es, es_ar and es_mx).

Checked: settings.xml is well formed and has the setting once, in that
group, with labels that exist; each strings file has the two ids once,
with the text of the core file. GUIDialogSettingsBase.cpp compiled
(syntax check). Not seen in the GUI.
